     ],
     "question": "Does the empirically measured scaling agree with the kernel's stated theoretical scaling within a factor of 2-3 across the sweep?",
     "reason": "Empirical scaling shows the predicted trend but with a 3-10x prefactor gap; paper discusses why.",
     "judge_reason": "The smaller-cutoff selector-cost trend appears, but preparation overhead substantially dilutes it. At the core reference, K falls from 32 to 18 while total gates fall only 8.96%, about 4.9 times less relative improvement than the cutoff reduction. The paper explains this through QSVT degrees and amplification. Larger-time slopes agree with unchanged leading time scaling, whereas precision exponents 0.916 and 0.920 do not demonstrate the asymptotic precision improvement.",
     "evidence": [
      "proposal/sections/discussion.tex:2–41",
      "proposal/logs/analysis.json, comparisons and fits",
      "proposal/sections/results.tex:61–74"
     ]
    },
